plan EntityDomains
title   Entity domains: coherent records, conservative repair and honest predictions
status  proposed
goal
    Plain operations get a declared shared-data meaning without author-written cache maintenance.
    One Runtime store keeps record fields coherent across domains. Machines own workflow; the
    domain records places, dependencies, predictions and server effects. Repair is conservative,
    bounded and explainable, and never predicts list membership. Operations are
    @plan.Operations.O1, hydration @plan.ServerRendering.O5 and Stories @plan.Testing.O1.
outcomes
    O1  Domains, records, repair and predictions
        An operation describes a request; a domain describes what it means for shared data. Terms: a
        record is one entity instance by key; an answer is one read entry's result for one input; a
        delta is a change to a record's confirmed base; a position orders sources by when their
        request started and landed; an answer's frontier is the newest position it reflects; a
        carrier is an active answer or loader whose shape holds a field. domain(id, entries) lists
        read(resource), write(transaction) and listen(stream) entries. An operation belongs to at
        most one domain and machines reach it only as O.Domain.entry; operation modules never import
        entities. Domains never send, commit or choose a state. A read needs a result Schema. An
        operation in two domains or two byKey loaders for one entity throw InvalidConfiguration at
        App construction or adoption; flow check reports them too, and warns on an unregistered
        operation whose result carries an entity's key fields.

        entity({ id, fields, primaryKey, version? }) declares fields and key. One entity object per
        id; a duplicate is a construction error. A read entry is built as read(resource) refined by
        methods: .one(Entity, at?) holds one record, .list(Entity, at, member?) holds records along
        a path, Entity.pick names an explicit shape, .byKey(...) marks the record loader, and
        .derivedFrom(Entity, fields?) declares values computed from records the response does not
        hold; omitted fields means any field, an empty list means membership only. Conditions use
        where (eq, oneOf, contains, between, overlaps, and) over record fields, input fields and
        constants. A condition about the signed-in account uses an input field the machine supplies,
        or serverMatches when only the server applies it. An absent optional input drops its
        constraint. Every list or derived input must appear in where, orderBy, pagedBy, ignoring or
        serverMatches, or construction fails; serverMatches names inputs whose filtering only the
        server knows.

        Order: orderBy ends with every entity key. A list is ordered, unordered or server-ranked
        (optionally narrowed by unaffectedBy). Order is the server's claim and the client never
        sorts. Each response is checked on its own number, boolean, date and key-field values, never
        strings, whose collation only the server knows; a conflict raises HiddenOrder naming the
        records, and that answer is treated as server-ranked for the rest of the Runtime.
        pagedBy(input, next) declares paging; entry.more loads the next page and the lane exposes
        hasMore. Repair re-fetches from page one up to the loaded count and publishes the whole
        chain at once, serving the old chain stale until then. A failed page keeps the previous
        chain, stale with the failure; a matching change mid-rebuild restarts it from page one.
        Extra list metadata has no repair guarantee (flow check warns); model totals as derived
        reads.

        Declarations are recorded once at construction. A position is one expression of property
        reads and library operators; ===, arithmetic, templates, method calls, conditionals,
        short-circuits and impure calls fail construction or flow check. predicts runs again at
        write start with the real input.

        Changes: predicts returns Entity.patch(key, fields) or Entity.delete(key, { version? }). A
        patch may only touch an entity named in a place or alsoChanges target of that entry; version
        fields cannot be patched, filtered or ordered by, so a version-only change never repairs.
        alsoChanges declares extra effects: Entity.all, Entity.where (answers whose conditions could
        overlap) or Entity.fields(key, fields), whose fields become unconfirmed: a confirming read
        is scheduled and lists repair only where they are condition or order fields. listen(stream)
        .as([Entities], mapping) may change only those entities. Call-site optimistic, setData and
        clear are rejected on domain operations; writes may only invalidate.

        One record per entity key holds received fields, each with its own version and position.
        Base is server-confirmed data; visible is base plus live predictions replayed over each new
        base. With versions the greater version wins, a lower one loses and an equal one falls
        through to position; by position, a field from a lookup that started before the stored value
        landed loses. Any response fills an absent field. A differing field that cannot be ordered
        is contested: its loader and holders refetch (or are only marked stale when the contest came
        from a repair's response) and the contest is recorded; Flow never merges. Predictions change
        visibility only and never start repairs. Two increments stay two increments; a later
        confirmed write of a field retires older predictions of it.

        Each base change is matched against every active answer in the App, whichever domain
        declared it, on its old or new values; a missing condition field matches. A list load that
        reveals records never held refetches derived answers on that entity once. A source rejected
        by ordering or a tombstone still joins derived answers, from its values to the current base;
        from a repair's response it only marks them stale. Repair rules: a one answer repairs only
        on delete; an ordered list repairs when a record enters or leaves or an order field of a
        record in it changes; an unordered list only on enter or leave; a server-ranked list also on
        any field change except unaffectedBy fields; a derived answer on enter or leave or a
        relevant field change. Merely displaying a field never needs repair. alsoChanges repairs
        every covered answer. Reconnect, restore and invalidate refetch covered answers that have
        subscribers, in canonical order, and mark the rest stale. A response behind the store for a
        record (a lower version) puts its answer out of date: from a machine's request the answer
        refetches; from a repair it is marked stale. A repair response updates copies and marks
        answers stale but never starts another lookup, so repair never cascades; the next machine
        request refreshes them. A request that started before an affecting change is stale on
        arrival. Each answer has one repair in flight and one trailing refetch. A settled write
        repairs for both the before-prediction and the at-settlement base, so an intervening push is
        never lost. Deltas are consolidated per record per turn but causes are never dropped.

        Write endings. Success installs response fields and ends confirmed predictions in one
        publication, so a value older than a settled write is never shown. Predicted fields the
        response omits stay pending until a confirming read (a later accepted read that carries the
        field) ends them even at an equal value. Settlement schedules one: an affected answer
        carrying the field that is already refetching, else the loader, else the first active answer
        carrying it, in canonical order. A read that omits the record confirms nothing, removes it
        from that answer and schedules the next candidate; a failed confirming read leaves the field
        pending until the next accepted read carrying it. If no active answer carries the field, the
        prediction ends and the field is dropped so the next reader fetches. Failure or defect
        removes predictions and repairs affected answers, because a failed call may have partly
        applied. Rejection or cancellation before the adapter ran removes predictions without
        repair. Uncertain (cancelled after invocation, response lost, or restored mid-flight) forces
        the loader and every holder to refetch, keeps predictions until that repair lands, then
        shows server state. The record stays uncertain until a later write to it succeeds or the
        machine reconciles the Attempt. A write restored as uncertain keeps its predicted keys with
        its Attempt, so restore marks them and forces the repair. unknown delivers an Attempt;
        O.x.retry(select) reissues that exact Attempt and a known-settled attempt settles without
        calling the adapter. basedOn gives each prediction's base version for If-Match. Persisted
        actors acknowledge the Attempt write before the adapter runs.

        Predicting writes queue per record, FIFO across actors, under actor concurrency (cancel is a
        flow check error). A multi-record write joins all its queues together and runs only when
        first in every one; predictions show while queued. Cancelling a queued, never-invoked write
        removes it. An uncertain write releases the next only after its forced repair settles,
        whether it succeeds or fails; the record stays uncertain.

        Creates return the record and repair matching answers; membership appears only when they
        refetch (no temporary ids or optimistic inserts). A predicted move changes fields at once
        but keeps list membership until repair. A predicted delete hides the record everywhere;
        failure restores it. A confirmed delete leaves a tombstone while lookups started before it
        are in flight, so they cannot resurrect it. A versioned delete keeps it until the record's
        gcTime and a save at a lower or equal version cannot bring the record back; flow check warns
        on listen over an unversioned entity. A non-list reference to a deleted record reads a typed
        Deleted value.

        Records are held while an answer, a loader subscription or a prediction needs them. Only
        confirmed data persists; no prediction is persisted, and restored data never overrides live
        truth. Answer reads are resource lanes plus stale; a failed repair keeps data as a failed
        refresh. Record reads are { data, pending, uncertain, unconfirmed }. value outcomes see
        confirmed data only, so a prediction never fires one and saved memory never captures one
        (@plan.Operations.O2).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/ticket.domain.ts.
    O2  Checkpoint reads and evidence
        Entity.get(snapshotOrCheckpoint, key, shape) reads exactly that cut: requested fields with
        data and status, missing fields marked missing, undefined if the record is absent.
        Domain.entry.get returns the lane plus stale and ordered pages, or undefined if no active
        answer exists. Entity.queue returns ordered attempts and queue states. Reads never touch
        live state, advance time or start work.

        Every checkpoint and run end carries repairs and skips for considered answers, plus
        contests, HiddenOrder and self-check findings, in the canonical order of @O5. In-process
        values, sensitive fields included, are faithful; exported evidence replaces sensitive values
        with keyed digests (@plan.Inspection.O5). Explaining a domain runs no adapter and uses the
        same repair classifier as the runtime (@plan.CommandLine.O1).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/queue/queue.story.ts.
    O3  Hydration and lazy domains
        Hydrated confirmed fields keep their versions; positions and frontiers stay local to each
        Runtime. Predictions, queues, tombstones and uncertain marks never transfer, and sensitive
        fields are omitted (@plan.ServerRendering.O3). Absent fields are filled. Merge precedence
        for fields, answers and predictions is @plan.ServerRendering.O5. Hydration never fires an
        initial value event.

        Joining a lazy domain happens at admission, before any turn sees it: ids, entity objects,
        registering domains and byKey loaders are checked against the adopted graph, structure and
        restored data publish as one cut, and failure rolls back everything (@plan.LazyFeatures.O2).
        A loader in an inactive entry is unavailable, so a field with no carrier is dropped; flow
        check warns.
    O4  propertyTest and audit mode
        propertyTest(domains, options) (@plan.Testing.O6) generates ordinary static Stories against
        fresh Runtimes: successful, failed and uncertain writes; reordered, duplicated, failed and
        empty reads; pushes; reconnect, restore, unsubscribe and invalidate; hydrate and mid-run
        domain adoption (@plan.Testing.O6). The fixture oracle holds its own server state, not the
        domain's rules. At every publication it checks: copies of a field agree; at quiet, active
        answers equal the oracle in declared order or are stale with causes; no repair-response
        lookup and every lookup has causes; no accepted field regresses; every displayed unconfirmed
        value is a live prediction with a scheduled or failed confirming read; a record leaves an
        answer only with a recorded reason; identical replays give identical traces; every repair,
        skip and hidden record has causes. Results are pass, fail or inconclusive, never
        inconclusive as success. Failures shrink to literal Stories with the seed.

        Audit is an explicit development and test option, off by default and never in production.
        After each settlement, push or observed change it refetches every active answer that was not
        repaired and compares without publishing: HiddenFilter reports a membership difference,
        UndeclaredEffect names the write, read and missing alsoChanges.
    O5  Bounds
        At most repairSlots repairs run at once (default 6, @plan.Runtime.O2), started in canonical
        order: delta, domain entry order, input key, then stable domain id. A paged repair of N
        loaded pages is N sequential requests in one slot. Each record queues at most 64 waiting
        writes; if any record of a multi-record write would overflow, the whole write is rejected
        before any prediction, queue join or adapter work, with AdmissionOverloaded naming the
        record and limit. Recorded constants and persisted data obey the structural limits of
        @plan.Inspection.O2. Production has no cross-turn execution budget.
decisions
    D1  Domains own shared-data policy
        Predictions replace call-site optimistic and setData; clear is forbidden and writes allow
        invalidate only.
constraints
    C1  Never invent server answers
        No predicted list membership, instant inserts, offline completeness, client-built query
        results, arbitrary predicate where, field merge, automatic uncertain retry or
        minimum-refetch promise. Machines own uncertain workflow.
    C2  Isolation
        One Runtime per scope and request; positions and frontiers never cross Runtimes; no
        prediction is persisted or hydrated; no version ever regresses.
verification
    V1  Authoring and types: unannotated inference of the reference domains; every rejected form
        (partial order, version in condition or patch, unused input, bad paging, undeclared patch
        entity, call-site optimistic) fails; source check rejects conditionals and impurity.
    V2  Repair properties: oracle agreement, coherence, no cascade and monotonic truth hold under
        propertyTest, and removing any one repair rule makes a named variant fail.
    V3  Prediction endings: confirming read (equal value, missing record, no carrier), partial
        failure repair, retirement of older predictions, and unversioned tombstone guard each have a
        targeted test.
    V4  Evidence: checkpoint reads, faithful in-process and digested export values, and impact
        tables agree with observed repairs; audit catches an omitted filter and an undeclared
        effect.
    V5  Hydrate, lazy join and bounds: A-to-B hydrate merges amid client writes, lazy adoption with
        rollback, 6 repair slots, N-request paged chains, and the 64/65 queue boundary with no
        partial joins.
questions
    Q1  Are 6 repair slots right?
        Recommended: measure Dispatch Desk page churn before changing the default.
    Q2  Deferred to build time
        Per-turn counters and exact event field lists; index structure for matching; tombstone gc
        accounting; evidence retention and sequence headroom; oracle evidence handoff.
